summary:
- topic: New Board Member Introductions
  text: The board welcomed new members Daniel Feller (District 1), George Risch (District
    4), and Tyler Smith (District 2), who each introduced themselves, shared their
    backgrounds, and expressed their commitment to supporting students and teachers.
- topic: Superintendent's Report
  text: The Superintendent's report included a heartfelt remembrance for longtime
    bus driver Bruce Cribby, recognition for South Portland Adult Education being
    named a state top performer, and an update on the Extended Learning Opportunities
    (ELO) program featuring a student presentation.
- topic: FY26 Financial Update and Spending Freeze
  text: The Finance Committee reported a significant potential budget deficit for
    the current fiscal year, ranging from $1.3 to $1.9 million. This projection, updated
    to include the ratified teachers' contract, prompted the administration to implement
    an immediate non-personnel spending freeze to ensure the district ends the year
    without a deficit.
- topic: Committee Reports
  text: The Policy Committee reported progress on revising policies for board member
    ethics, public comment, and temperature control. The Buildings & Grounds Committee
    is reviewing the temperature policy and the ongoing issue with the unusable athletic
    field at the middle school was raised again by board members.
- topic: Community Donations
  text: The board gratefully accepted a total of $46,583.10 in donations and grants
    from various organizations, including the Bridge to Work Foundation, Donors Choose,
    and Hannaford Helps Schools, to support numerous school programs, classroom resources,
    and student activities across the district.
- topic: Policy Approvals
  text: The board gave final approval to Policy IGDJ (Co-Curricular Activities) and
    accepted a first reading of a revised Policy BCA (School Board Member Ethics),
    which was edited to read as a policy rather than a personal oath.
- topic: Public Comment
  text: Citizens and the SPTA president offered support, asked clarifying questions
    about the budget deficit's origins and the impact of the spending freeze on classrooms
    and substitute availability, and reiterated community interest in establishing
    a district education foundation.
